Review operating evidence recovery handover and discipline entry (@engineering-operation-review: 0, is #chapter) { r_00F5C065E1FD91E52024353E0C
Explain a person's task as part of the engineering function. Connect intended output with material, energy and information paths, actual configuration, operating conditions and support. Distinguish the control action, acknowledgement, physical transition, observation, interpretation and evidence of successful service. Reuse the computing account of intent and authority without mistaking a software receipt for a measured physical result.

Defend the finite card-board model: state meanings, event preconditions, nondeterministic ticks, exact observations, conservation, buffer capacity and preserved finished output. Derive the ambiguous pending/arrived pair and its permitted-command intersection. Explain why a full state snapshot can become stale, why an empty possible-state set is a model/evidence conflict and why no liveness guarantee follows from allowing progress.

Produce a recovery table and the source-based handover reconstruction. Justify the minimal observation-field result within its assumptions, then explain why a real handover needs more than those fields. Compare a slip, a mistaken model and a deliberate workaround as rival explanations requiring different evidence. Propose a design revision and an appropriate evaluation without inventing error probabilities or participant findings.

Transfer the account to a harmless document or paper assembly task with one partially completed item and one unresolved instruction. State the available information, authority, next actions and expected evidence. Change the configuration during a handover and show exactly which earlier claim needs rechecking. Explain what demonstration, independent attempt and changed-condition performance could support about the recipient's capability, and what they cannot support.

Use [Connect operating evidence to the major engineering disciplines](Connect-operating-evidence-to-the-major-engineering-disciplines.lingua#r_00E446205CFF984E3AE1329789) to connect civil, chemical, materials, mechanical/electrical and production questions to the shared core. Preserve their full theoretical, experimental and practical scope. The common account prepares entry; it does not complete human factors, systems engineering, reliability, actual commissioning or the engineering core. Continue the oriented-field mathematics and remaining magnetic, spacetime/gravity and astronomy bridges before the cross-area readiness review and major-branch development.

**Return:** [Engineering](../Engineering.lingua#r_5DA6VY09MAD2N8D9SH0FFJVV68).
